  "bookOsisID": "Lam",
  "bookName": "Lamentations",
  "importedAt": "2025-11-25",
  "commentary": [
    {
      "sequence": 10044,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.1",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.1.1",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 1:1",
        "long": "Lamentations 1:1"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.1.1-22\">1:1-22</a></span> Chapter 1 mourns the destruction and desolation of Jerusalem, recognizing that this was God’s well-deserved judgment on Israel’s sins.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10045,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.1",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.1.2",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 1:2",
        "long": "Lamentations 1:2"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.1.2\">1:2</a></span> The <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">lovers</span> were the fertility deities, Baal and Asherah, who were worshiped by most people in Judah. Her <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">friends</span> were her military allies, notably Egypt, who was no match for Babylon (<a href=\"?bref=Jer.37.5-11\">Jer 37:5-11</a>).</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10046,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.1",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.1.3",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 1:3",
        "long": "Lamentations 1:3"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.1.3\">1:3</a></span> The woman of <a href=\"?bref=Lam.1.1\">1:1</a> is the nation of <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">Judah</span>. • <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">led away into captivity:</span> This happened four times: 605 <span class=\"era\">BC</span> (<a href=\"?bref=Dan.1.3-4\">Dan 1:3-4</a>); 597 <span class=\"era\">BC</span> (<a href=\"?bref=2Kgs.24.12-16\">2 Kgs 24:12-16</a>;  <a href=\"?bref=Jer.52.28\">Jer 52:28</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Ezek.1.2-3\">Ezek 1:2-3</a>); 586 <span class=\"era\">BC</span> (<a href=\"?bref=2Kgs.25.5-7\">2 Kgs 25:5-7</a>, <a href=\"?bref=2Kgs.25.18-21\">18-21</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Jer.39.5-10\">Jer 39:5-10</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Jer.52.29\">52:29</a>); and 581 <span class=\"era\">BC</span> (<a href=\"?bref=Jer.52.30\">Jer 52:30</a>).</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10047,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.1",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.1.4",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 1:4",
        "long": "Lamentations 1:4"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.1.4\">1:4</a></span> <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">Jerusalem</span> (Hebrew <span class=\"ital\">Zion</span>): In the Psalms and in the prophetic books, Zion represents the city of Jerusalem. In Lamentations, Zion is a poetic name for the city, even in ruins. <a href=\"?bref=Heb.12.22-24\">Hebrews 12:22-24</a> speaks of the heavenly Zion (see also <a href=\"?bref=Rev.14.1-5\">Rev 14:1-5</a>). • The annual <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">festivals</span> in Jerusalem were the Passover, Unleavened Bread, First Harvest, Harvest (Pentecost), Trumpets, the Day of Atonement, and Shelters (Tabernacles). • The main duties of the <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">priests</span> were carried out in the Temple and its surrounding area. Since the Temple was in ruins, they had no jobs and hence no future.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10048,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.1",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.1.5",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 1:5",
        "long": "Lamentations 1:5"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.1.5\">1:5</a></span> <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">for her many sins:</span> Judah’s sins before the Babylonian invasion are outlined in <a href=\"?bref=2Kgs.21.1-9\">2 Kgs 21:1-9</a>, <a href=\"?bref=2Kgs.21.16\">16</a>; <a href=\"?bref=2Kgs.24.3-4\">24:3-4</a> (cp. <a href=\"?bref=2Chr.33.1-10\">2 Chr 33:1-10</a>, <a href=\"?bref=2Chr.33.22-23\">22-23</a> and <a href=\"?bref=2Chr.36.11-16\">2 Chr 36:11-16</a>). Similarly, a list of Israel’s sins before Assyria destroyed it is found in <a href=\"?bref=2Kgs.17.14-23\">2 Kgs 17:14-23</a>.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10049,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.1",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.1.7",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 1:7",
        "long": "Lamentations 1:7"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.1.7\">1:7</a></span> <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">laughed as she fell:</span> See also <a href=\"?bref=Jer.24.9\">Jer 24:9</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Ezek.22.5\">Ezek 22:5</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Ezek.36.4\">36:4</a>.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10050,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.1",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.1.8",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 1:8",
        "long": "Lamentations 1:8"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.1.8-9\">1:8-9</a></span> <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">Jerusalem</span> was <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">stripped</span> of everything of physical and spiritual value that the people cherished.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10051,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.1",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.1.10",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 1:10",
        "long": "Lamentations 1:10"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.1.10\">1:10</a></span> For historical accounts of these events, see <a href=\"?bref=2Kgs.25.8-17\">2 Kgs 25:8-17</a>; <a href=\"?bref=2Chr.36.13-21\">2 Chr 36:13-21</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Jer.52.4-30\">Jer 52:4-30</a>.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10052,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.1",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.1.13",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 1:13",
        "long": "Lamentations 1:13"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.1.13\">1:13</a></span> The destruction of Jerusalem at the hands of the Babylonians was like <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">fire from heaven</span>.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10053,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.1",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.1.14",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 1:14",
        "long": "Lamentations 1:14"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.1.14\">1:14</a></span> For their <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">sins</span>, the people of Judah were tied to one another with <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">ropes</span> and taken as captives into exile.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10054,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.1",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.1.15",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 1:15",
        "long": "Lamentations 1:15"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.1.15\">1:15</a></span> <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">his beloved city:</span> God had given special care to Jerusalem, like a father protecting his daughter. But her sins had been persistent, and her punishment was horrible. • <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">like grapes . . . trampled in a winepress:</span> This common procedure for extracting juice from grapes is used vividly in <a href=\"?bref=Isa.63.3\">Isa 63:3</a> to show the horrors of punishment. In <a href=\"?bref=Rev.14.18-20\">Rev 14:18-20</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Rev.19.15\">19:15</a>, the image graphically represents universal judgment.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10055,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.1",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.1.19",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 1:19",
        "long": "Lamentations 1:19"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.1.19\">1:19</a></span> <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">I begged my allies:</span> Egypt had a great deal of interest in Judah because Judah controlled the only good route to the north. However, Egypt turned her back on Judah when Babylon attacked Jerusalem (<a href=\"?bref=Jer.37.5-11\">Jer 37:5-11</a>). • When there was little rain in the fall and spring in Palestine, a summer without rain could destroy crops and cause a food shortage. The food supply also ran out under the prolonged siege of a city (Jan 588–July 586 <span class=\"era\">BC</span>), and many people <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">starved to death</span> (<a href=\"?bref=2Kgs.25.2-3\">2 Kgs 25:2-3</a>; cp. <a href=\"?bref=Jer.37.21\">Jer 37:21</a>).</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10056,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.1",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.1.20",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 1:20",
        "long": "Lamentations 1:20"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.1.20\">1:20</a></span> The leaders and people of Judah had <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">rebelled</span> (<a href=\"?bref=Jer.2.17\">Jer 2:17</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Jer.5.6\">5:6</a>, <a href=\"?bref=Jer.5.23\">23</a>) by rejecting the one true God and his commandments and by resisting the invaders God had sent (<a href=\"?bref=2Kgs.24.1\">2 Kgs 24:1</a>, <a href=\"?bref=2Kgs.24.20\">20</a>; <a href=\"?bref=2Chr.36.13\">2 Chr 36:13</a>).</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10057,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.1",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.1.21",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 1:21",
        "long": "Lamentations 1:21"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.1.21\">1:21</a></span> <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">the day you promised:</span> The poet’s spirit was heartened by the belief that God would set up a day when everyone—oppressors and victims—would be judged justly (see <a href=\"?bref=Isa.13.6\">Isa 13:6</a>, <a href=\"?bref=Isa.13.9\">9</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Jer.46.10-12\">Jer 46:10-12</a>, <a href=\"?bref=Jer.46.27-28\">27-28</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Amos.5.18-20\">Amos 5:18-20</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Obad.1.15\">Obad 1:15</a>; <a href=\"?bref=1Thes.5.2-3\">1 Thes 5:2-3</a>; <a href=\"?bref=2Thes.2.2\">2 Thes 2:2</a>).</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10058,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.2",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.2.1",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 2:1",
        "long": "Lamentations 2:1"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.2.1-22\">2:1-22</a></span> This chapter graphically portrays the Lord as Jerusalem’s destroyer.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10059,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.2",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.2.1",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 2:1",
        "long": "Lamentations 2:1"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.2.1\">2:1</a></span> <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">the heights of heaven:</span> Jerusalem’s buildings were splendid, adorned with precious metals and jewels. Jerusalem had the reputation of being the sacred city of the Lord. • <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">his Temple</span> (literally <span class=\"ital\">his footstool</span>): Cp. <a href=\"?bref=1Chr.28.2\">1 Chr 28:2</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Ps.99.5\">Pss 99:5</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Ps.132.7\">132:7</a>.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10060,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.2",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.2.3",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 2:3",
        "long": "Lamentations 2:3"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.2.3-4\">2:3-4</a></span> <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">bends his bow:</span> In the past, the Lord had been a mighty warrior on Israel’s behalf (see <a href=\"?bref=Exod.14.13-15.21\">Exod 14:13–15:21</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Josh.23.9-11\">Josh 23:9-11</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Judg.7.1-25\">Judg 7</a>; <a href=\"?bref=2Kgs.19.32-35\">2 Kgs 19:32-35</a>); now he waged war against Jerusalem (see <a href=\"?bref=Isa.63.10\">Isa 63:10</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Ezek.24.25\">Ezek 24:25</a>).</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10061,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.2",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.2.6",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 2:6",
        "long": "Lamentations 2:6"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.2.6\">2:6</a></span> A <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">garden shelter</span> was a temporary structure that harvesters put up to provide shade. God had promised to take care of <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">his Temple</span> forever (<a href=\"?bref=1Kgs.9.3\">1 Kgs 9:3</a>), but now it seemed that he had abandoned it.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10062,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.2",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.2.7",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 2:7",
        "long": "Lamentations 2:7"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.2.7\">2:7</a></span> The Temple was destroyed because the sins that the priests and the people had committed within its walls had defiled its holiness (cp. <a href=\"?bref=Jer.7.3-15\">Jer 7:3-15</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Jer.26.3-6\">26:3-6</a>, <a href=\"?bref=Jer.26.12-13\">12-13</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Ezek.24.21\">Ezek 24:21</a>).</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10063,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.2",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.2.8",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 2:8",
        "long": "Lamentations 2:8"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.2.8\">2:8</a></span> <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">Ramparts</span> were stone walls or mounds of earth built around a city to protect it from assault by an enemy army.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10064,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.2",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.2.9",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 2:9",
        "long": "Lamentations 2:9"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.2.9\">2:9</a></span> <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">no more visions:</span> Daniel and Ezekiel were <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">prophets</span> of the Lord who had visions while they lived among the exiles far from the land of Judah (<a href=\"?bref=Ezek.1.1\">Ezek 1:1</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Dan.7.1\">Dan 7:1</a>). This verse refers to prophets in Jerusalem who claimed to have visions that proved to be false.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10065,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.2",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.2.10",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 2:10",
        "long": "Lamentations 2:10"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.2.10\">2:10</a></span> <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">Burlap</span> (or <span class=\"ital\">sackcloth</span>) was made of goat or camel hair and was often used for grain sacks or for items to be carried on pack animals. Poor people wore it as clothing because it was inexpensive, and people in mourning wore it as a sign of their deep sorrow (its dark color matched their mood, and its roughness matched their discomfort). • Throwing <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">dust on their</span> <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">heads</span> and clothing was a sign to neighbors that a family member had died.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10066,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.2",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.2.13",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 2:13",
        "long": "Lamentations 2:13"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.2.13\">2:13</a></span> <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">Who can heal you?</span> Reputed healers and prophets were all liars and frauds (<a href=\"?bref=Jer.6.13-14\">Jer 6:13-14</a>).</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10067,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.2",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.2.16",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 2:16",
        "long": "Lamentations 2:16"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.2.16\">2:16</a></span> Their <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">enemies</span> were glad that Jerusalem was defeated.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10068,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.2",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.2.17",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 2:17",
        "long": "Lamentations 2:17"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.2.17\">2:17</a></span> <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">promises . . . made long ago:</span> God’s promises of blessing were always contingent upon Israel’s obedience to the law (<a href=\"?bref=Deut.8.1-20\">Deut 8</a>). He had promised to destroy them if they disobeyed it (<a href=\"?bref=Lev.26.1-46\">Lev 26</a>).</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10069,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.2",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.2.18",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 2:18",
        "long": "Lamentations 2:18"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.2.18-19\">2:18-19</a></span> Repentance and <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">prayer</span> are the proper responses when facing the devastation of God’s judgment.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10070,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.2",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.2.20",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 2:20",
        "long": "Lamentations 2:20"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.2.20\">2:20</a></span> Jeremiah had predicted that the same cannibalism that occurred during a siege of Samaria (<a href=\"?bref=2Kgs.6.28-29\">2 Kgs 6:28-29</a>) would happen in Jerusalem (<a href=\"?bref=Jer.19.9\">Jer 19:9</a>; see also <a href=\"?bref=Lam.4.10\">Lam 4:10</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Ezek.5.10\">Ezek 5:10</a>; cp. <a href=\"?bref=Deut.28.53-57\">Deut 28:53-57</a>).</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10071,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.2",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.2.22",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 2:22",
        "long": "Lamentations 2:22"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.2.22\">2:22</a></span> <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">calling them to a day of feasting:</span> The poet compares Jerusalem’s destruction to an invitation to a banquet at which war, famine, wild animals, and sickness would feast on the wicked in Jerusalem (<a href=\"?bref=Deut.28.15-68\">Deut 28:15-68</a>; cp. <a href=\"?bref=Rev.19.17-21\">Rev 19:17-21</a>).</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10072,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.3",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.3.1",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 3:1",
        "long": "Lamentations 3:1"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.3.1-66\">3:1-66</a></span> In this chapter, the author laments what has happened (<a href=\"?bref=Lam.3.1-20\">3:1-20</a>, <a href=\"?bref=Lam.3.48-54\">48-54</a>), remembers the faithful love of the Lord (<a href=\"?bref=Lam.3.21-25\">3:21-25</a>), describes how God’s people should respond (<a href=\"?bref=Lam.3.26-47\">3:26-47</a>), and calls upon the Lord in prayer (<a href=\"?bref=Lam.3.55-66\">3:55-66</a>).</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10073,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.3",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.3.1",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 3:1",
        "long": "Lamentations 3:1"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.3.1-24\">3:1-24</a></span> The author speaks of the suffering of Judah and Jerusalem as his own.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10074,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.3",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.3.6",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 3:6",
        "long": "Lamentations 3:6"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.3.6-9\">3:6-9</a></span> Some understand these verses as a poetic reference to Jeremiah’s confinement in a muddy cistern before the destruction of Jerusalem (<a href=\"?bref=Jer.38.6-13\">Jer 38:6-13</a>).</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10075,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.3",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.3.9",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 3:9",
        "long": "Lamentations 3:9"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.3.9\">3:9</a></span> <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">blocked my way . . . made my road crooked:</span> Sin causes confusion and cuts a person or a community off from a happy future. Innocent people know the future as a straight path that is easy to follow (<a href=\"?bref=Pr.21.8\">Prov 21:8</a>). Isaiah imagined God’s activities as a procession on a straight road that his worshipers would prepare (<a href=\"?bref=Isa.40.3\">Isa 40:3</a>).</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10076,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.3",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.3.12",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 3:12",
        "long": "Lamentations 3:12"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.3.12\">3:12</a></span> Cp. <a href=\"?bref=Job.6.4\">Job 6:4</a>.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10077,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.3",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.3.19",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 3:19",
        "long": "Lamentations 3:19"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.3.19\">3:19</a></span> <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">is bitter beyond words</span> (or <span class=\"ital\">is wormwood and gall</span>): Wormwood is a plant with a bitter taste; here it represents the emotional intensity of inner agony (<a href=\"?bref=Pr.5.4\">Prov 5:4</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Rev.8.11\">Rev 8:11</a>). Gall is a poisonous plant that causes severe physical pain if eaten; it is a powerful symbol for extremely stressful emotions (<a href=\"?bref=Deut.29.18\">Deut 29:18</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Ps.69.21\">Ps 69:21</a>). The author seems to be at a dead end from which he cannot escape.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10078,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.3",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.3.22",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 3:22",
        "long": "Lamentations 3:22"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.3.22-33\">3:22-33</a></span> God’s love and faithfulness <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">never cease</span>. Just as God had been faithful in bringing judgment on Jerusalem for their sins, he would be faithful in bringing restoration to those who returned to him.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10079,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.3",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.3.22",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 3:22",
        "long": "Lamentations 3:22"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.3.22\">3:22</a></span> The <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">faithful love of the</span> <span class=\"sn-excerpt-divine-name\">Lord</span> is the basis for the poet’s recovery from deep depression. As with Jeremiah in the cistern (<a href=\"?bref=Jer.38.6-13\">Jer 38:6-13</a>) and Jonah in the stomach of the great fish (<a href=\"?bref=Jon.2.2-10\">Jon 2:2-10</a>), the Lord provided salvation from death.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10080,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.3",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.3.23",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 3:23",
        "long": "Lamentations 3:23"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.3.23\">3:23</a></span> God’s <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">faithfulness</span> speaks of his absolute reliability, which is evident in his daily <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">mercies</span>. He continually provides a habitable world in which we can live.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10081,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.3",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.3.24",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 3:24",
        "long": "Lamentations 3:24"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.3.24\">3:24</a></span> <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">The</span> <span class=\"sn-excerpt-divine-name\">Lord</span><span class=\"sn-excerpt\"> is my inheritance:</span> The land of Canaan had been regarded as Israel’s inheritance since the time of Moses (<a href=\"?bref=Exod.15.17\">Exod 15:17</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Josh.21.19\">Josh 21:19</a>; <a href=\"?bref=1Chr.28.8\">1 Chr 28:8</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Ps.47.4\">Ps 47:4</a>), but the true inheritance of God’s people is really God himself (see <a href=\"?bref=Ps.16.5-6\">Ps 16:5-6</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Eph.1.11\">Eph 1:11</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Heb.9.15\">Heb 9:15</a>; <a href=\"?bref=1Pet.1.3-4\">1 Pet 1:3-4</a>).</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10082,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.3",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.3.26",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 3:26",
        "long": "Lamentations 3:26"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.3.26\">3:26</a></span> Those who are confident of God’s plan can <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">wait quietly</span> for him to grant <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">salvation</span>.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10083,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.3",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.3.28",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 3:28",
        "long": "Lamentations 3:28"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.3.28\">3:28</a></span> <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">sit alone in silence:</span> Humble submission stops the tongue and quiets the heart.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10084,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.3",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.3.29",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 3:29",
        "long": "Lamentations 3:29"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.3.29-30\">3:29-30</a></span> In the ancient Near East, lying <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">face down in the dust</span> expressed submission (<a href=\"?bref=Gen.17.1-3\">Gen 17:1-3</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Lev.9.24\">Lev 9:24</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Josh.7.6\">Josh 7:6</a>; <a href=\"?bref=1Sam.5.4\">1 Sam 5:4</a>; <a href=\"?bref=1Kgs.18.39\">1 Kgs 18:39</a>; <a href=\"?bref=1Chr.21.16\">1 Chr 21:16</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Matt.17.5-6\">Matt 17:5-6</a>). • To <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">turn the other cheek</span> also expresses submission. Jesus evidently had this verse in mind when he taught his disciples to submit to persecution (<a href=\"?bref=Matt.5.39\">Matt 5:39</a>). This response recognizes that God is sovereign even over our suffering.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10085,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.3",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.3.31",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 3:31",
        "long": "Lamentations 3:31"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.3.31\">3:31</a></span> Cp. <a href=\"?bref=Ps.103.8-11\">Ps 103:8-11</a>.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10086,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.3",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.3.34",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 3:34",
        "long": "Lamentations 3:34"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.3.34-36\">3:34-36</a></span> The people of Judah were doing such things before Jerusalem was destroyed (see <a href=\"?bref=Jer.5.26-31\">Jer 5:26-31</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Jer.21.11-14\">21:11-14</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Jer.23.10-14\">23:10-14</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Mic.3.1-12\">Mic 3</a>).</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10087,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.3",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.3.37",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 3:37",
        "long": "Lamentations 3:37"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.3.37-39\">3:37-39</a></span> Some calamities have natural causes (<a href=\"?bref=Luke.13.1-5\">Luke 13:1-5</a>), and bad things happen to the righteous as well as to the wicked (<a href=\"?bref=Matt.5.45\">Matt 5:45</a>); whatever happens, we should give thanks (<a href=\"?bref=1Thes.5.18\">1 Thes 5:18</a>) and not <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">complain</span>.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10088,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.3",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.3.40",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 3:40",
        "long": "Lamentations 3:40"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.3.40-42\">3:40-42</a></span> Repentance is the key to receiving salvation (<a href=\"?bref=Isa.1.27\">Isa 1:27</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Jer.3.22\">Jer 3:22</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Ezek.3.21\">Ezek 3:21</a>).</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10089,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.3",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.3.48",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 3:48",
        "long": "Lamentations 3:48"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.3.48-66\">3:48-66</a></span> The author focuses on the viciousness of the enemies and cries out to the Lord.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10090,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.3",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.3.52",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 3:52",
        "long": "Lamentations 3:52"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.3.52-57\">3:52-57</a></span> This passage might refer to Jeremiah’s experience in the cistern (<a href=\"?bref=Jer.37.11-15\">Jer 37:11-15</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Jer.38.1-13\">38:1-13</a>).</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10091,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.3",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.3.58",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 3:58",
        "long": "Lamentations 3:58"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.3.58-66\">3:58-66</a></span> Jerusalem deserved punishment, but the <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">enemies</span> carried it out with undeserved cruelty. The writer calls upon God to punish them.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10092,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.3",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.3.64",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 3:64",
        "long": "Lamentations 3:64"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.3.64-66\">3:64-66</a></span> This prayer for vengeance is similar to several psalms (see “<a href=\"?item=PrayersForVengeance_ThemeNote_Filament\">Prayers for Vengeance</a>” Theme Note).</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10093,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.4",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.4.1",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 4:1",
        "long": "Lamentations 4:1"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.4.1-22\">4:1-22</a></span> Although God’s people still experience his judgment, they will soon experience restoration.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10094,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.4",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.4.3",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 4:3",
        "long": "Lamentations 4:3"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.4.3\">4:3</a></span> <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">like ostriches:</span> See also <a href=\"?bref=Job.39.13-16\">Job 39:13-16</a>.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10095,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.4",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.4.7",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 4:7",
        "long": "Lamentations 4:7"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.4.7\">4:7</a></span> <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">like fine jewels:</span> (literally <span class=\"ital\">like lapis lazuli</span>): Lapis lazuli is a beautiful blue stone that is soft enough to carve. It is often used in decorations and mosaics.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10096,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.4",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.4.8",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 4:8",
        "long": "Lamentations 4:8"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.4.8\">4:8</a></span> <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">Skin</span> sticking to <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">bones</span> is symptomatic of the final stages of starvation, just before death.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10097,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.4",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.4.9",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 4:9",
        "long": "Lamentations 4:9"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.4.9\">4:9</a></span> Long sieges result in a serious <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">lack of food</span>. Even if people could get to the fields, they would find the crops destroyed or harvested to feed the soldiers.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10098,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.4",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.4.12",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 4:12",
        "long": "Lamentations 4:12"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.4.12\">4:12</a></span> <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">Not a king . . . could march through the gates of Jerusalem:</span> Since God had delivered Jerusalem from Sennacherib of Assyria more than a century earlier (<a href=\"?bref=2Kgs.19.36-37\">2 Kgs 19:36-37</a>), Judeans had strongly believed that not even the mightiest king, Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon, could defeat the city.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10099,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.4",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.4.15",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 4:15",
        "long": "Lamentations 4:15"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.4.15\">4:15</a></span> It appears that these leaders <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">fled</span> from Jerusalem as refugees to other <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">lands</span>.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10100,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.4",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.4.20",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 4:20",
        "long": "Lamentations 4:20"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.4.20\">4:20</a></span> <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">Our king . . . was caught:</span> A reference to Zedekiah, who tried to flee but was caught and treated cruelly by Nebuchadnezzar (<a href=\"?bref=2Kgs.25.4-7\">2 Kgs 25:4-7</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Jer.39.4-7\">Jer 39:4-7</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Jer.52.9-11\">52:9-11</a>).</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10101,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.4",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.4.21",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 4:21",
        "long": "Lamentations 4:21"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.4.21-22\">4:21-22</a></span> The people of <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">Edom</span> were feeling secure and gloating over Jerusalem’s misfortune, but they, too, would experience <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">punishment</span> for their <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">sins</span> (see <a href=\"?bref=Obad.1.1-21\">Obad 1</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Jer.49.7-22\">Jer 49:7-22</a>).</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10102,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.4",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.4.21",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 4:21",
        "long": "Lamentations 4:21"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.4.21\">4:21</a></span> <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">Uz</span> was an area east of the Jordan River that extended south to Edom. It was Job’s home (<a href=\"?bref=Job.1.1\">Job 1:1</a>).</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10103,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.4",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.4.22",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 4:22",
        "long": "Lamentations 4:22"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.4.22\">4:22</a></span> The first <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">return from exile</span> occurred in 538 <span class=\"era\">BC</span>, after Cyrus of Persia defeated Babylon (<a href=\"?bref=2Chr.36.22-23\">2 Chr 36:22-23</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Ezra.1.1-4\">Ezra 1:1-4</a>).</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10104,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.5",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.5.1",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 5:1",
        "long": "Lamentations 5:1"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.5.1-22\">5:1-22</a></span> This chapter is a heartfelt prayer for restoration (cp. <a href=\"?bref=Dan.9.4-19\">Dan 9:4-19</a>).</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10105,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.5",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.5.10",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 5:10",
        "long": "Lamentations 5:10"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.5.10\">5:10</a></span> There was a <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">famine</span> in the city during the siege (<a href=\"?bref=2Kgs.25.3\">2 Kgs 25:3</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Jer.37.21\">Jer 37:21</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Jer.38.9\">38:9</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Jer.52.6\">52:6</a>).</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10106,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.5",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.5.12",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 5:12",
        "long": "Lamentations 5:12"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.5.12-17\">5:12-17</a></span> These terrible things happened <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">because</span> the people had <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">sinned</span> (see <a href=\"?bref=Isa.24.7-12\">Isa 24:7-12</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Jer.7.24\">Jer 7:24</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Jer.13.18\">13:18</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Jer.25.10-11\">25:10-11</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Amos.8.10\">Amos 8:10</a>).</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10107,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.5",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.5.18",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 5:18",
        "long": "Lamentations 5:18"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.5.18\">5:18</a></span> <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">haunted by jackals:</span> Cp. <a href=\"?bref=Isa.13.22\">Isa 13:22</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Isa.34.13\">34:13</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Jer.9.11\">Jer 9:11</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Jer.10.22\">10:22</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Mal.1.3\">Mal 1:3</a>.</p>"
    },
    {
      "sequence": 10108,
      "bookOsisID": "Lam",
      "chapterOsisID": "Lam.5",
      "verseOsisID": "Lam.5.22",
      "citation": {
        "short": "Lam 5:22",
        "long": "Lamentations 5:22"
      },
      "html": "<p class=\"sn-text\"><span class=\"sn-ref\"><a href=\"?bref=Lam.5.22\">5:22</a></span> <span class=\"sn-excerpt\">have you utterly rejected us?</span> The tragedy of the Exile raised the question of whether God would forgive sin and restore his people. God answered clearly through the prophets (<a href=\"?bref=Isa.40.27-31\">Isa 40:27-31</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Isa.41.8-20\">41:8-20</a>) and by sending Christ (<a href=\"?bref=Matt.9.2-6\">Matt 9:2-6</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Matt.26.28\">26:28</a>; <a href=\"?bref=Luke.24.47\">Luke 24:47</a>).</p>"
    }
  ]
}